    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the breakdown. so you said phosphorus is for roots... but how do i actually see if that's the issue? i dont want to just guess and throw more nitrogen on if the phosphorus is what's actually missing. are there specific signs in the leaves or something?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Phosphorus can be a bit more difficult to detect, but there are some signs that can indicate a phosphorus deficiency. Look for symptoms like stunted growth, thin, wiry stems, or sparse new growth. Forcing new shoots to grow can also help reveal phosphorus issues, as the shoots will emerge with a light-colored base and a darker, thicker stem – an area about 1-2 inches above the soil, a paler color can indicate a nitrogen issue, but crisp and dark would suggest phosphorus deficiency.",
